      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-white mb-3">DediRock VPS Review 2026: i9 Single-Core from $24.88/yr</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-400 text-base">Last updated: August 24, 2026 · 8 min read</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-gray-300 leading-relaxed space-y-5">

      <p>The budget-VPS conversation on this site usually revolves around AMD EPYC and Intel Xeon unmanaged KVM boxes at rock-bottom monthly prices — and for good reason, since those deliver the best compute-per-dollar when you are comfortable running your own server. But in August 2026 a newer budget player, <strong>DediRock</strong>, rolled out an Intel <strong>i9-powered VPS line</strong> that takes a different angle: instead of chasing the absolute lowest nominal price, it leans into higher <strong>single-core performance</strong> on newer DDR5 + NVMe hardware. We pulled the live pricing and specs from dedirock.com, cross-checked them against the LowEndBox announcement, and compared the lineup against the VPSTier-covered budget hosts to see where DediRock actually fits.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">DediRock at a Glance</h2>
      <p>DediRock LLC is a budget hosting brand positioned for single-core-heavy and storage-heavy workloads. Its two verified US data center locations are <strong>Buffalo, New York</strong> (the BUF1 facility in Western New York, which DediRock describes as upstate infrastructure with strong latency to NYC, Chicago, and Toronto, plus favorable connectivity to Europe) and <strong>Los Angeles, California</strong>. The August 2026 refresh introduced the <strong>i9 Dream™</strong> VPS lineup — announced on <a href="https://lowendbox.com/blog/live-the-i9-dream-dedirocks-i9-powered-deals-are" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline" rel="noopener" target="_blank">LowEndBox</a> in mid-August — which DediRock pitches for database scaling, rendering, virtualization, and resource-heavy applications that benefit from a fast single thread. It also runs a storage-focused VPS line ("Storage Wars") and has an AMD Ryzen VPS line announced as coming soon.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">The i9 Dream line — why single-core matters</h2>
      <p>Most budget VPS at this price point runs older or lower-clocked AMD EPYC / Intel Xeon vCPUs with modest single-thread throughput. That is fine for web servers and general workloads, but it hurts for <strong>gaming servers, application builds, rendering, and latency-sensitive single-threaded tasks</strong>. DediRock's bet is that a segment of cheap-VPS buyers wants the opposite: a fast per-core experience even at a slightly higher nominal entry price. The i9 Dream line pairs Intel i9 vCores with <strong>DDR5 RAM and NVMe SSD</strong> on a 1 Gbps network port — a genuinely different hardware stack than the typical budget tier.</p>
      <p>We verified the official numbers on the dedirock.com i9 Dream page in August 2026. The three plans, all billed annually with 1 dedicated IPv4:</p>

