      </div>
      <p>If your workload is a Minecraft or game server, a render farm node, or a single-threaded app where one fast core is the bottleneck, DediRock's i9 line is worth weighing against RackNerd's bare-budget tier. If you need hourly elasticity and global anycast reach, Vultr remains the reference. DediRock is a <strong>specific-value</strong> pick, not a universal one.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Who should choose DediRock — and who shouldn't</h2>
      <p>DediRock is a <strong>specific-value</strong> pick, and it is worth being explicit about the two sides of that coin:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-300">
        <li><strong>Choose DediRock if</strong> you run a game server (Minecraft, FiveM, or similar), a build/render node, a database that is bottlenecked on a single thread, or any latency-sensitive single-threaded workload where one fast core matters more than total cores. The i9 Dream entry at ~$2.07/mo is a strong per-dollar single-core offer, and annual billing removes the renewal-price whiplash you get with some monthly intro deals.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose DediRock if</strong> you need cheap bulk storage for backups or large files and want RAID 5/6 redundancy you are not paying for elsewhere — the Storage line at $12.88/yr for 256 GB is among the lowest per-GB rates we track.</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid DediRock if</strong> you need hour-by-hour elasticity, a broad global anycast footprint, or true month-to-month billing. The i9 plans are annual-only, the datacenter count is two US locations (Buffalo + LA), and a newer host like this simply has a shorter operational track record than the established budget leaders.</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid DediRock if</strong> your workload is bulk random I/O (e.g. a busy object-store or OLTP database): the storage line is explicitly not for heavy 4K random read/write, and the compute line tops out at consumer-oriented i9 vCores rather than dedicated SSD-optimized IOPS.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>And a note on the AMD Ryzen line: DediRock announced "The Ryzen Experience™" as part of its roadmap, with AMD Ryzen VPS plans coming soon. If you prefer AMD over Intel, it may be worth watching DediRock for the Ryzen launch rather than committing solely on the i9 line today.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Our Verdict</h2>
      <p>DediRock earns a <strong>4.0/5</strong> for what it is: a genuinely differentiated budget provider that sells fast single-core compute on annual terms rather than racing to the lowest nominal monthly price. The i9 Dream entry at <strong>$24.88/year</strong> with 2 GB DDR5 + 30 GB NVMe is compelling for gaming and single-thread-heavy workloads, and the RAID 5/6 storage line adds redundancy at a good per-GB rate. The trade-offs — annual-only billing, a two-location US footprint, and a shorter track record as a newer host — make it a fit for a specific job, not a blanket recommendation over the established budget leaders.</p>
      <p>For month-to-month budget flexibility, see our <a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd review</a>. For hourly-scaled cloud across 32+ locations, see <a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a>. DediRock is a strong addition to the shortlist when single-core performance matters more than nominal price.</p>

